      "id": 1,
      "text": "Laguna Niguel's name blends Spanish \"laguna\" for lagoon with Acjachemem village \"Niguili\" near Aliso Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_Niguel%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "First recorded residents were the Acjachemem Native Americans, who may have had a village called \"Niguili\" near Aliso Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_Niguel%2C_California",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1959, the Laguna Niguel Corporation bought Rancho Niguel land to create one of California's first master‑planned communities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_Niguel%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Completion of Interstate 5 in 1959 opened easy access to Los Angeles, sparking a housing boom in Orange County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laguna_Niguel%2C_California",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Laguna Niguel is served by OCTA bus lines 85, 87, 90, 91, 187, and 490, connecting residents to regional hubs.",
